  INSERT INTO learning_content
    (level, topic_order, topic_name, sub_concept_order, sub_concept_name, explanation, check_question, check_answer)
  VALUES
    (2, 1, 'Investing Basics', 1, 'Why Invest?', 'Keeping money in a savings account or under your mattress feels safe, but it has a hidden cost: that money doesn''t grow. Investing means putting your money into something — a business, a fund, a property — with the expectation that it will be worth more later. The point isn''t to gamble; it''s to let your money work alongside you instead of sitting still.', 'What''s the main difference between saving and investing?', 'Saving keeps money safe but largely unchanged. Investing puts money to work with the expectation (not guarantee) of growth over time.'),
    (2, 1, 'Investing Basics', 2, 'Why Saving Alone Isn''t Enough', 'If you save ₹10,000 in a regular savings account earning close to nothing, and prices in the economy rise by 6% a year, your ₹10,000 buys less a year from now even though the number in your account hasn''t dropped. Saving protects the number; investing aims to protect — and grow — what that number can actually buy.', 'Can your savings balance go up in numbers but still lose value in real terms?', 'Yes — if the interest you earn is lower than inflation, your money''s purchasing power shrinks even as the account balance grows.'),
    (2, 1, 'Investing Basics', 3, 'How Investments Grow', 'Investments grow through a few basic mechanisms: a business becomes more valuable and its shares are worth more (capital appreciation), a company shares its profits with you (dividends), or a lender pays you interest for the use of your money (bonds, fixed deposits). Different investments grow in different ways, and at different speeds.', 'Name two different ways an investment can grow in value.', 'Any two of: capital appreciation (the asset itself becomes worth more), dividends/interest (regular payouts), or rental income (for property).'),
    (2, 1, 'Investing Basics', 4, 'Investment Myths', 'A common myth is that investing is only for the wealthy or for people who understand complex charts. In reality, many investment options today start with small, regular amounts, and the core ideas — patience, consistency, understanding what you own — matter more than insider knowledge or large capital.', 'True or false — you need a large lump sum to start investing.', 'False. Many investment vehicles allow small, regular contributions (like a monthly SIP) rather than requiring a large upfront amount.'),
    (2, 1, 'Investing Basics', 5, 'Wealth vs Income', 'Income is what flows in regularly — your salary, freelance earnings, rental payments. Wealth is what you''ve accumulated and kept — savings, investments, property, minus what you owe. A high income doesn''t automatically mean high wealth if all of it is spent; a moderate income with consistent saving and investing can build more wealth over time.', 'Can someone with a high income have low wealth?', 'Yes — if they spend most or all of their income rather than saving or investing a portion of it, their wealth stays low regardless of how much they earn.'),
    (2, 2, 'Compound Interest', 1, 'Simple Interest', 'Simple interest is calculated only on your original amount, every time, regardless of how long you''ve invested. If you put in ₹10,000 at 10% simple interest, you earn ₹1,000 every year — the same ₹1,000, because the calculation never looks at what you''ve already earned.', 'After 3 years at 10% simple interest on ₹10,000, how much total interest have you earned?', '₹3,000 (₹1,000 × 3 years, since simple interest never grows on itself)'),
    (2, 2, 'Compound Interest', 2, 'Compound Interest', 'Compound interest is calculated on your original amount plus whatever interest you''ve already earned. That same ₹10,000 at 10% compound interest earns ₹1,000 in year one (total ₹11,000), then 10% of ₹11,000 — ₹1,100 — in year two (total ₹12,100). The interest itself starts earning interest.', 'Why does compound interest grow faster than simple interest over time?', 'Because each year''s interest is calculated on a bigger number than the year before, while simple interest is always calculated on the same original amount.'),
    (2, 2, 'Compound Interest', 3, 'Rule of 72', 'A quick mental shortcut: divide 72 by your annual return rate to estimate how many years it takes your money to double. At 12% annual return, 72 ÷ 12 = 6 years to double. At 8%, it takes 9 years. This isn''t exact, but it''s a fast way to compare how rate affects doubling time.', 'At a 9% annual return, roughly how many years would it take to double your money?', 'About 8 years (72 ÷ 9 = 8)'),
    (2, 2, 'Compound Interest', 4, 'Power of Starting Early', 'Someone who invests ₹5,000/month starting at age 25 and stops at 35 (10 years of contributions) can end up with more money by retirement than someone who starts at 35 and contributes the same monthly amount all the way to 60 — because the first person''s money had far longer to compound, even with fewer years of actual contributions.', 'Why can an early, short investing period sometimes outperform a later, longer one?', 'Because the earlier money has more years to compound, and compounding accelerates over time — the extra years of growth matter more than extra years of contributing.'),
    (2, 2, 'Compound Interest', 5, 'Time vs Amount', 'When you''re short on time, you often try to compensate with a larger amount invested. But because compounding is exponential, not linear, time is usually the more powerful lever of the two — doubling your monthly contribution rarely makes up for losing a decade of compounding.', 'If you can''t increase how much you invest each month, what''s the next most powerful thing you can do to build wealth?', 'Start as early as possible, since time is the factor compounding depends on most.'),
    (2, 3, 'Time Value of Money', 1, 'Present Value', '₹1,000 today is worth more than ₹1,000 a year from now, because money today can be invested and grow. Present value is the idea of working backward: figuring out what a future amount of money is actually worth to you right now, given that it could have been growing in the meantime.', 'Why is ₹1,000 today generally considered more valuable than ₹1,000 received a year from now?', 'Because the ₹1,000 you have today can be invested and start growing immediately, while the future ₹1,000 hasn''t had that chance yet.'),
    (2, 3, 'Time Value of Money', 2, 'Future Value', 'Future value flips the question around: if you invest a certain amount today at a given rate of return, what will it be worth at some point in the future? This is the same math behind compound interest — it''s just asking "where does this end up" instead of "where did this start."', 'If future value tells you what an investment grows into, what does present value tell you?', 'Present value tells you what a future amount is worth in today''s terms — essentially the reverse calculation.'),
    (2, 3, 'Time Value of Money', 3, 'Why Time Matters', 'Every year your money isn''t invested is a year it isn''t compounding. This is why "I''ll start investing later, once I have more money" often costs more in lost growth than waiting actually saves — the lost time can matter more than the size of the amount you delayed investing.', 'What''s the real cost of delaying investing, even if you eventually invest the same total amount?', 'The lost compounding time — money invested later has fewer years to grow, even if the amount invested is identical.'),
    (2, 3, 'Time Value of Money', 4, 'Inflation vs Growth', 'For an investment to actually build wealth, it needs to grow faster than inflation erodes purchasing power. An investment returning 5% when inflation is running at 6% is technically growing in number, but losing value in what it can actually buy.', 'If an investment returns 4% in a year where inflation is 6%, has your real wealth grown or shrunk?', 'Shrunk in real terms — even though the number grew by 4%, prices rose faster (6%), so your purchasing power decreased.'),
    (2, 4, 'Risk & Return', 1, 'Risk', 'Risk is the chance that an investment''s actual outcome differs from what you expected — including the chance of losing money. Different investments carry different levels and types of risk: a fixed deposit has very low risk of losing your principal, while a stock''s price can swing significantly in either direction.', 'Does "risk" only mean the chance of losing money?', 'Not exactly — risk more broadly means uncertainty in outcomes, which includes the possibility of earning less than expected, not just losing money outright.'),
    (2, 4, 'Risk & Return', 2, 'Return', 'Return is what you actually earn (or lose) on an investment, usually expressed as a percentage of what you put in. It''s the flip side of risk: investments that offer the potential for higher returns generally come with higher uncertainty about whether you''ll actually get that return.', 'If two investments promise very different potential returns, what should that difference make you curious about?', 'The difference in risk — a much higher promised return usually comes with meaningfully higher risk or uncertainty, and is worth questioning rather than assuming it''s "free" extra reward.'),
    (2, 4, 'Risk & Return', 3, 'Volatility', 'Volatility describes how much an investment''s value moves up and down over time, even if it ends up in a similar place eventually. A highly volatile investment might be worth 20% more one month and 15% less the next, even if its long-term trend is upward.', 'Can an investment be volatile but still profitable over the long run?', 'Yes — volatility describes the up-and-down movement along the way, not necessarily the eventual long-term outcome.'),
    (2, 4, 'Risk & Return', 4, 'Reward', 'Reward is the potential benefit that justifies taking on a particular risk. The general relationship in investing is that taking on more risk is usually necessary to have the potential for greater reward — there''s rarely a way to get meaningfully higher expected returns without accepting more uncertainty.', 'Is it generally possible to get significantly higher returns with zero added risk?', 'No — higher potential returns are generally tied to higher risk; an offer that claims otherwise is worth being skeptical of.'),
    (2, 4, 'Risk & Return', 5, 'Long-term Perspective', 'Short-term price swings can look alarming, but many forms of risk and volatility tend to smooth out over longer time periods. A long-term perspective means evaluating an investment''s role in your goals over years, not reacting to day-to-day or month-to-month movements.', 'Why might checking your investments every single day make investing feel riskier than it actually is?', 'Because daily price movements can be noisy and emotionally distracting, even when the long-term trend is what actually matters for your goals.'),
    (2, 5, 'Financial Goals', 1, 'Short-term Goals', 'Short-term goals are things you want to achieve within the next 1-2 years — an emergency fund, a phone upgrade, a short trip. Because the timeline is short, this money generally shouldn''t be exposed to high volatility, since you may not have time to recover from a downturn before you need the funds.', 'Why might a highly volatile investment be a poor fit for a goal that''s only 6 months away?', 'Because there''s little time to recover if the value drops right before you need the money — short timelines need more stability, not higher potential returns.'),
    (2, 5, 'Financial Goals', 2, 'Medium-term Goals', 'Medium-term goals typically fall in the 2-7 year range — a car, a wedding, a course fee. These can sometimes tolerate a bit more risk than short-term goals, since there''s more time to ride out fluctuations, but still benefit from a degree of caution as the goal date approaches.', 'Should your approach to a medium-term goal change as the target date gets closer?', 'Often yes — many people shift toward more stable options as the goal date nears, to protect against a downturn right before they need the money.'),
    (2, 5, 'Financial Goals', 3, 'Long-term Goals', 'Long-term goals stretch beyond 7-10 years — retirement, a child''s higher education, building long-term wealth. These goals can generally tolerate more short-term volatility, because there''s enough time for growth-oriented investments to recover from downturns and compound meaningfully.', 'Why can long-term goals generally afford to take on more short-term risk than short-term goals?', 'Because a longer time horizon gives more room to recover from temporary downturns and benefit from long-term compounding.'),
    (2, 5, 'Financial Goals', 4, 'SMART Goals', 'A useful way to define financial goals is to make them Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound. "I want to save more" is vague; "I want to save ₹2,00,000 for a wedding in 3 years" is specific enough to actually plan around.', 'What''s missing from the goal "I want to invest for retirement"?', 'Specificity and a timeframe — there''s no clear amount, target date, or measurable milestone to plan toward.'),
    (2, 5, 'Financial Goals', 5, 'Goal Prioritization', 'Most people have multiple financial goals competing for the same limited money. Prioritizing means deciding which goals matter most or are most urgent — an emergency fund and high-interest debt typically come before longer-term goals, since they protect you from setbacks that could derail everything else.', 'Why might building an emergency fund come before investing for a long-term goal?', 'Because without a safety buffer, an unexpected expense could force you to withdraw long-term investments early, possibly at a loss, disrupting the long-term plan.'),
    (2, 6, 'Asset Classes (Introduction)', 1, 'Stocks', 'A stock represents partial ownership in a company. When you own a stock, you own a small slice of that business, and its value tends to rise and fall with how the company (and the market''s perception of it) performs.', 'What does owning a stock actually represent?', 'Partial ownership in a company — not a loan to the company, but a share of the business itself.'),
    (2, 6, 'Asset Classes (Introduction)', 2, 'Bonds', 'A bond is essentially a loan — you lend money to a government or company, and they agree to pay you back with interest over a set period. Bonds are generally considered more stable than stocks, though they''re not entirely risk-free.', 'How is owning a bond different from owning a stock?', 'A bond makes you a lender (you''re owed money plus interest), while a stock makes you a part-owner of a business.'),
    (2, 6, 'Asset Classes (Introduction)', 3, 'Gold', 'Gold has historically been viewed as a store of value, particularly during times of economic uncertainty. It doesn''t pay interest or dividends — its potential return comes only from its price changing over time.', 'Does gold pay you interest or dividends while you hold it?', 'No — gold''s only potential source of return is a change in its price; it doesn''t generate income on its own.'),
    (2, 6, 'Asset Classes (Introduction)', 4, 'Cash', 'Cash (and cash-equivalents like savings accounts) is the most liquid and stable asset class — easy to access immediately, but typically offering little to no growth, and vulnerable to losing purchasing power to inflation over time.', 'What''s the main trade-off of holding a large portion of your money as cash?', 'High accessibility and stability, but little to no growth — and a real risk of losing purchasing power to inflation over time.'),
    (2, 6, 'Asset Classes (Introduction)', 5, 'Real Estate', 'Real estate involves owning physical property, with potential returns coming from rental income, the property''s value appreciating, or both. It typically requires more capital to start and is far less liquid than stocks or cash. *(No product details yet — this is introduced further in Level 3.)*', 'Name one reason real estate is considered less liquid than stocks.', 'Selling property generally takes much longer and involves more steps (legal, financial, finding a buyer) than selling a stock, which can often be done almost instantly.'),
    (2, 7, 'Investment Psychology', 1, 'Patience', 'Investment growth, particularly through compounding, often takes years to become significant. Patience means staying invested through the unglamorous, slow-feeling middle years rather than abandoning a plan because progress doesn''t feel fast enough.', 'Why might the first few years of investing feel slower or less rewarding than later years?', 'Because compounding effects are smaller in absolute terms early on and become more visible as the invested amount grows — patience is required before the growth becomes more noticeable.'),
    (2, 7, 'Investment Psychology', 2, 'Greed', 'Greed in investing often shows up as chasing whatever investment is currently performing best, hoping to capture quick, outsized gains — sometimes ignoring the actual risk involved or buying in after most of the gains have already happened.', 'What''s a common warning sign that greed, rather than a sound plan, is driving an investment decision?', 'Making a decision primarily because something "is going up right now," rather than because it fits your goals, timeline, and risk tolerance.'),
    (2, 7, 'Investment Psychology', 3, 'Fear', 'Fear often causes people to sell investments during a downturn, locking in a loss that might have recovered if they''d stayed invested. It can also stop people from investing at all, out of worry about losing money, even when staying entirely in cash carries its own risk (like inflation).', 'How can fear-driven selling during a downturn turn a temporary loss into a permanent one?', 'By selling at a low point, you lock in that loss — if you''d stayed invested, the value might have recovered, but once sold, that recovery no longer benefits you.'),
    (2, 7, 'Investment Psychology', 4, 'Emotional Decisions', 'Many investing mistakes come not from lack of knowledge, but from emotional reactions in the moment — panic-selling during a dip, or excitedly over-investing during a rally. Recognizing when emotion (not analysis) is driving a decision is a skill in itself.', 'Is it usually a lack of financial knowledge, or something else, that causes most investing mistakes?', 'Often something else — emotional reactions like fear and greed frequently drive poor decisions even among people who understand the underlying concepts well.'),
    (2, 7, 'Investment Psychology', 5, 'Long-term Thinking', 'Long-term thinking means evaluating decisions based on how they serve your multi-year goals, not how they feel in a single day or week. It''s the psychological discipline that makes patience, and resistance to fear and greed, actually possible to practice.', 'How does long-term thinking help counter both fear and greed?', 'By keeping the focus on multi-year goals rather than short-term price movements, it becomes easier to ignore the emotional pull of a single bad day (fear) or a single hot trend (greed).'),
    (2, 8, 'Investment Mistakes', 1, 'Waiting Too Long', 'Many people delay investing while waiting to "feel ready" — more knowledge, more savings, a better moment. Because time is one of the most powerful factors in compounding, this waiting period has a real, often underestimated cost.', 'What''s the hidden cost of waiting until you feel "fully ready" to start investing?', 'Lost compounding time — every year of delay is a year your money isn''t growing, and that lost time is difficult to make up for later.'),
    (2, 8, 'Investment Mistakes', 2, 'Chasing Returns', 'Chasing returns means moving your money toward whatever investment recently performed the best, assuming it will keep performing that way. Past performance doesn''t guarantee future results, and by the time an investment''s strong performance is widely known, much of the opportunity may have already passed.', 'Why is buying into something because "it''s been going up a lot recently" considered risky?', 'Because past performance doesn''t guarantee future performance, and a recent strong run may mean the price is already high, with the easy gains already captured by earlier investors.'),
    (2, 8, 'Investment Mistakes', 3, 'Timing the Market', 'Trying to time the market means attempting to buy right before prices rise and sell right before they fall. This is extremely difficult to do consistently, even for professionals, and getting it wrong (in either direction) can cost more than simply staying invested through ups and downs.', 'Why is consistently timing the market considered very difficult, even for experienced investors?', 'Because it requires correctly predicting short-term price movements, which are influenced by countless unpredictable factors — being right occasionally is very different from being right consistently.'),
    (2, 8, 'Investment Mistakes', 4, 'Ignoring Inflation', 'Treating a "safe" 0% or low-return option as risk-free ignores the fact that inflation steadily erodes purchasing power. An option that protects the number in your account isn''t actually safe if it quietly loses you real value every year.', 'Can a "guaranteed" investment still result in a loss of real value?', 'Yes — if its return is lower than the inflation rate, you lose purchasing power over time even though the account balance never goes down.'),
    (2, 8, 'Investment Mistakes', 5, 'No Goals', 'Investing without a clear goal makes it hard to choose appropriate investments or know when you''re on track. Without a target, it''s also harder to resist chasing trends or panicking at volatility, since there''s no defined plan to anchor your decisions against.', 'Why does having a specific goal make it easier to stay disciplined during a market downturn?', 'Because a clear goal and timeline give you a reference point to evaluate whether you''re still on track, rather than reacting purely to short-term price movements.'),
    (3, 1, 'Stocks', 1, 'What are Stocks?', 'A stock represents a small ownership stake in a company. When a company issues stock, it''s essentially selling tiny pieces of itself to raise money, and each piece (share) entitles the holder to a proportional claim on the company''s value and future profits.', 'When you buy a share of stock, what are you actually buying?', 'A small ownership stake in that company — not a loan or a promise of fixed returns, but a proportional claim on the business itself.'),
    (3, 1, 'Stocks', 2, 'Ownership', 'As a shareholder, you own a fraction of the company, which can come with certain rights, like voting on major company decisions, though for very small individual holdings this influence is often minimal in practice. Your gain or loss comes from how the value of that ownership stake changes over time, plus any profit-sharing the company chooses to distribute.', 'Does owning one share of a large company typically give an individual investor significant control over its decisions?', 'No — while shareholders technically have voting rights proportional to their holding, a single small investor''s individual shares usually represent a tiny fraction of the total, giving limited practical influence over company decisions.'),
    (3, 1, 'Stocks', 3, 'Stock Exchanges', 'A stock exchange is a regulated marketplace (like the NSE or BSE in India) where shares of publicly listed companies are bought and sold. Exchanges provide a transparent, standardized way for buyers and sellers to trade, with prices determined by ongoing supply and demand.', 'Why does a stock''s price change throughout the trading day, even if nothing about the company itself has changed?', 'Because stock prices are driven by ongoing buying and selling activity on the exchange — shifts in investor sentiment, expectations, or market-wide factors can move prices even without new company-specific news.'),
    (3, 1, 'Stocks', 4, 'Dividends', 'A dividend is a portion of a company''s profit distributed directly to shareholders, usually on a regular schedule. Not all companies pay dividends — some choose to reinvest all profits back into growing the business instead, which can also benefit shareholders through a higher stock price over time.', 'If a company doesn''t pay dividends, does that mean shareholders get no benefit from the company''s profits?', 'Not necessarily — a company that reinvests profits instead of paying dividends may grow faster, which can increase the value of the stock itself, benefiting shareholders through price appreciation rather than direct payouts.'),
    (3, 1, 'Stocks', 5, 'Capital Appreciation', 'Capital appreciation is the increase in a stock''s price over time, separate from any dividends received. This is often the primary way investors aim to profit from stocks — buying at one price with the expectation that the company''s growth, and therefore the stock''s value, will rise.', 'What''s the difference between earning money from a stock through dividends versus through capital appreciation?', 'Dividends are direct cash payments from the company''s profits, paid while you continue to hold the stock. Capital appreciation is the increase in the stock''s own price, which only becomes a realized gain when you actually sell.'),
    (3, 2, 'Mutual Funds', 1, 'What are Mutual Funds?', 'A mutual fund pools money from many investors and uses it to buy a diversified collection of assets — stocks, bonds, or both — on their behalf. This lets individual investors gain instant diversification and professional management without needing to pick and manage individual securities themselves.', 'Why might a mutual fund be appealing to someone who doesn''t have the time or expertise to pick individual stocks?', 'Because a mutual fund is professionally managed and automatically diversified across many holdings, giving investors broad market exposure without needing to research and select individual securities themselves.'),
    (3, 2, 'Mutual Funds', 2, 'Fund Managers', 'A fund manager is the professional (or team) responsible for deciding what the fund buys and sells, based on the fund''s stated strategy and objectives. Their decisions directly affect the fund''s performance, which is part of why some funds with different managers can perform quite differently even with similar stated goals.', 'Does every mutual fund following the same general strategy (like "large-cap equity") perform identically?', 'No — fund manager decisions, specific holdings, and timing can all differ between funds even within the same broad category, leading to different performance outcomes.'),
    (3, 2, 'Mutual Funds', 3, 'NAV', 'Net Asset Value (NAV) is the per-unit price of a mutual fund, calculated by dividing the total value of everything the fund holds by the number of units outstanding. When you invest in a mutual fund, you''re buying units at the current NAV, and your investment''s value moves as the NAV moves.', 'If a mutual fund''s NAV rises from ₹50 to ₹55, what has happened to the value of units you already hold?', 'Their value has increased proportionally — if you held units worth ₹50 each, they''re now worth ₹55 each, reflecting the fund''s overall growth in value.'),
    (3, 2, 'Mutual Funds', 4, 'Types of Funds', 'Mutual funds vary widely by what they invest in and how — equity funds (mostly stocks), debt funds (mostly bonds/fixed income), and hybrid funds (a mix of both) are some of the broadest categories, each carrying different risk and return characteristics suited to different goals and timelines.', 'Would a debt fund or an equity fund typically be considered higher risk?', 'An equity fund is generally considered higher risk, since stock prices tend to be more volatile than the fixed-income instruments a debt fund typically holds — though equity funds also carry higher long-term growth potential.'),
    (3, 2, 'Mutual Funds', 5, 'Expense Ratio', 'The expense ratio is the annual fee a mutual fund charges, expressed as a percentage of your investment, to cover management and operating costs. Even a seemingly small difference in expense ratio can meaningfully affect your returns over many years, since it''s deducted every year regardless of the fund''s performance.', 'Why might a 1% difference in expense ratio matter more than it initially sounds like, over a 20-year investment?', 'Because that 1% is deducted every single year, compounding its impact over time — over two decades, the cumulative effect on your total returns can be substantial, even though the annual difference seems small.'),
    (3, 3, 'ETFs & Index Funds', 1, 'ETF Basics', 'An Exchange-Traded Fund (ETF) is similar to a mutual fund in that it holds a diversified basket of assets, but it trades on a stock exchange throughout the day like an individual stock, rather than being bought or sold only at the end of the day at a single NAV.', 'What''s a key structural difference between how you buy a mutual fund versus how you buy an ETF?', 'Mutual fund units are typically bought/sold at the end-of-day NAV, while ETFs trade continuously on an exchange throughout the day, with prices that can fluctuate during trading hours like a stock.'),
    (3, 3, 'ETFs & Index Funds', 2, 'Index Funds', 'An index fund is designed to replicate the performance of a specific market index (like the Nifty 50) by holding the same securities in the same proportions, rather than having a fund manager actively pick which stocks to buy. The goal is to match the index''s performance, not to try to beat it.', 'Is an index fund trying to outperform the market, or simply match it?', 'Simply match it — an index fund''s goal is to replicate the performance of its target index as closely as possible, not to try to beat it through active stock selection.'),
    (3, 3, 'ETFs & Index Funds', 3, 'Passive Investing', 'Passive investing means following a fund strategy (like an index fund) that doesn''t involve a manager actively trying to pick winning investments — instead, it simply tracks a predefined index or rule. This approach typically comes with lower fees, since there''s less active research and decision-making involved.', 'Why do passive index funds generally have lower expense ratios than actively managed funds?', 'Because passive funds simply track an index using a predefined formula, requiring far less ongoing research, analysis, and active decision-making than actively managed funds, which reduces operating costs.'),
    (3, 3, 'ETFs & Index Funds', 4, 'Tracking Index', 'The specific index a fund tracks (like the Nifty 50, Sensex, or a sector-specific index) determines what that fund actually invests in and how its performance will move. Two index funds tracking different indices can have very different risk and return profiles, even though both are "passive."', 'If two index funds track different indices, should you expect them to perform similarly?', 'Not necessarily — performance depends on what the underlying index actually contains; different indices can represent very different segments of the market with different risk and return characteristics.'),
    (3, 4, 'Fixed Income', 1, 'Fixed Deposits', 'A Fixed Deposit (FD) is a bank product where you deposit a lump sum for a fixed period in exchange for a guaranteed interest rate, known in advance. FDs are widely used for their predictability and low risk, though they generally offer lower potential returns than market-linked investments like stocks.', 'What''s the main trade-off of choosing a Fixed Deposit over a market-linked investment?', 'Predictability and low risk, in exchange for generally lower potential returns compared to market-linked investments like stocks or equity mutual funds over the long term.'),
    (3, 4, 'Fixed Income', 2, 'Bonds', 'A bond is a loan you give to a government or company, which agrees to pay you periodic interest and return your principal at a set maturity date. Bonds are generally considered less risky than stocks, though the actual risk level varies depending on who issued the bond and their financial stability.', 'Are all bonds equally safe?', 'No — bond risk depends heavily on the issuer''s creditworthiness; a bond from a financially stable government is typically considered much safer than a bond from a financially weaker company.'),
    (3, 4, 'Fixed Income', 3, 'Government Securities', 'Government securities (G-Secs) are debt instruments issued by the government to fund its spending, generally considered among the safest fixed-income investments available since they''re backed by the government itself. They typically offer lower yields than corporate bonds, reflecting their lower risk.', 'Why might a government security offer a lower interest rate than a similar corporate bond?', 'Because government securities are generally considered lower-risk (less likely to default), and lower risk is typically associated with lower potential returns — investors accept a smaller yield in exchange for greater safety.'),
    (3, 4, 'Fixed Income', 4, 'Treasury Bills', 'Treasury bills (T-bills) are short-term government debt instruments, typically maturing in less than a year, sold at a discount to their face value rather than paying periodic interest. They''re often used by investors seeking very short-term, low-risk parking of funds.', 'How does a T-bill''s return work, given that it doesn''t pay periodic interest like a typical bond?', 'You buy it at a discount to its face value and receive the full face value at maturity — the difference between the discounted purchase price and the face value is your return.'),
    (3, 5, 'Gold', 1, 'Physical Gold', 'Physical gold — jewellery, coins, or bars — is the most traditional form of gold ownership in India, but it comes with practical costs: making charges, storage and security concerns, and a price gap between buying and selling (the difference between what a jeweller charges you and what they''d pay to buy it back).', 'Besides the gold''s market price, what other costs are involved in buying physical gold jewellery?', 'Making charges (for crafting the jewellery) and a buy-sell price gap — when you eventually sell, you typically get less than the current market price for the gold content alone, due to these added costs.'),
    (3, 5, 'Gold', 2, 'Digital Gold', 'Digital gold lets you buy and sell gold in small amounts online, with the gold typically held in secure vaults by the provider on your behalf. It removes the storage and security concerns of physical gold, though it isn''t government-regulated in the same way as some other gold investment options.', 'What''s one practical advantage of digital gold over physical gold?', 'It removes the need for personal storage and security, since the actual gold is held in secure vaults by the provider rather than at your home.'),
    (3, 5, 'Gold', 3, 'Gold ETF', 'A Gold ETF is an exchange-traded fund that holds gold (or gold-related assets) and trades on a stock exchange like a stock, giving you exposure to gold''s price movements without needing to physically hold or store gold yourself.', 'How is investing in a Gold ETF different from buying physical gold?', 'A Gold ETF gives you price exposure to gold through a tradeable financial instrument, without the costs and hassle of physical storage, security, or making charges associated with owning actual gold.'),
    (3, 5, 'Gold', 4, 'Sovereign Gold Bonds', 'Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGBs) are government-issued bonds denominated in grams of gold, offering both the price movement of gold and a fixed additional interest rate paid by the government, typically with a multi-year tenure. They''ve historically been considered a tax-efficient way to gain gold exposure if held to maturity.', 'What''s one feature of Sovereign Gold Bonds that pure gold ETFs or physical gold don''t offer?', 'A fixed additional interest payment from the government on top of any gold price appreciation — physical gold and most Gold ETFs only offer exposure to gold''s price movement, without a separate interest component.'),
    (3, 6, 'Government Investments', 1, 'PPF', 'The Public Provident Fund (PPF) is a long-term, government-backed savings scheme currently offering 7.1% annual interest (as of FY 2026-27), with a 15-year lock-in period, and contributions between ₹500 and ₹1.5 lakh per financial year. It carries "EEE" tax status — meaning the contribution, the interest earned, and the final maturity amount are all tax-exempt (contribution deduction available only under the older tax regime).', 'What does "EEE" tax status mean for an investment like PPF?', 'Exempt-Exempt-Exempt: the amount you contribute can be tax-deductible, the interest earned each year is tax-free, and the final maturity amount is also tax-free — all three stages are exempt from tax.'),
    (3, 6, 'Government Investments', 2, 'EPF', 'The Employees'' Provident Fund (EPF) is a retirement savings scheme for salaried employees, where both the employee and employer contribute a percentage of salary each month, with interest credited annually. It''s mandatory for many salaried employees in India and builds a retirement corpus over the course of a career.', 'How does EPF differ from PPF in terms of who contributes?', 'EPF involves contributions from both the employee and the employer, while PPF is funded entirely through the individual''s own voluntary contributions, with no employer involvement.'),
    (3, 6, 'Government Investments', 3, 'NPS', 'The National Pension System (NPS) is a voluntary, market-linked retirement savings scheme where contributions are invested across a mix of equity, corporate debt, and government securities (in proportions you can often influence), with the goal of building a retirement corpus, part of which is typically used to purchase an annuity for regular pension income after retirement.', 'How is NPS different from PPF in terms of how returns are generated?', 'NPS returns are market-linked, since contributions are invested in equity and debt instruments whose value can fluctuate, while PPF offers a fixed, government-declared interest rate that doesn''t depend on market performance.'),
    (3, 6, 'Government Investments', 4, 'Sukanya Samriddhi', 'The Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana is a government savings scheme specifically designed for the benefit of a girl child, offering a government-set interest rate (historically often higher than PPF) and tax benefits, intended to help build savings for her education or marriage-related expenses over time.', 'Who is the Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana specifically designed for?', 'It''s a savings scheme specifically created for the financial benefit of a girl child, typically opened and operated by a parent or guardian on her behalf.'),
    (3, 6, 'Government Investments', 5, 'NSC', 'The National Savings Certificate (NSC) is a fixed-income government savings instrument with a multi-year maturity period and a government-set interest rate, offering tax deduction benefits on the amount invested (under the older tax regime) and is generally used for safe, medium-term savings goals.', 'Is NSC a market-linked investment or a fixed-return investment?', 'Fixed-return — like PPF, NSC offers a government-declared interest rate that doesn''t fluctuate with market performance, making it a low-risk, predictable savings option.'),
    (3, 7, 'Real Estate Basics', 1, 'Property', 'Owning property (residential or commercial) is one of the most capital-intensive investment options, typically requiring a large upfront amount or a long-term loan. It''s also one of the least liquid investments, since selling property generally takes significant time and involves legal and procedural steps.', 'Why is real estate generally considered a less liquid investment than stocks or mutual funds?', 'Because selling property typically takes much longer — involving finding a buyer, legal documentation, and registration — compared to selling stocks or mutual fund units, which can often be done almost immediately.'),
    (3, 7, 'Real Estate Basics', 2, 'Rental Income', 'Rental income is the regular payment a property owner receives from tenants in exchange for the use of the property. It can provide a steady income stream, though it comes with responsibilities like maintenance, vacancy risk (periods with no tenant), and dealing with tenant-related issues.', 'Is rental income guaranteed once you own a rental property?', 'No — rental income depends on consistently finding and retaining tenants; vacancy periods, maintenance costs, and tenant issues can all affect the actual income received.'),
    (3, 7, 'Real Estate Basics', 3, 'Appreciation', 'Property appreciation refers to an increase in the property''s market value over time, influenced by factors like location, infrastructure development, and overall demand. Unlike rental income, appreciation is only realized as an actual gain when the property is eventually sold.', 'If your property''s market value has risen, have you actually "made money" from that increase yet?', 'Not until you sell — appreciation represents an increase in paper value, but the gain only becomes realized (actual money in hand) once the property is actually sold at that higher value.'),
    (3, 7, 'Real Estate Basics', 4, 'REIT', 'A Real Estate Investment Trust (REIT) allows investors to gain exposure to income-generating real estate (like commercial buildings) by buying units that trade on a stock exchange, without needing to directly purchase, manage, or finance physical property themselves.', 'How does a REIT make real estate investing more accessible compared to buying property directly?', 'It allows investment in real estate with much smaller amounts of money, through exchange-traded units, without the large capital requirement, illiquidity, and management responsibilities of owning physical property directly.'),
    (3, 8, 'Choosing Products', 1, 'Matching Goals', 'Different financial products suit different goals. A short-term goal generally pairs better with lower-risk, more liquid options, while a long-term goal can often accommodate products with more growth potential but also more short-term fluctuation, like equity-oriented investments.', 'Why wouldn''t a 15-year lock-in product like PPF be a good fit for a goal you need to fund in 2 years?', 'Because the money would be inaccessible for far longer than your actual timeline — a product''s lock-in period needs to align with when you''ll actually need the funds.'),
    (3, 8, 'Choosing Products', 2, 'Liquidity', 'Liquidity refers to how quickly and easily an investment can be converted to cash without a significant loss in value. Stocks and many mutual funds are generally quite liquid; real estate and certain government schemes with long lock-ins are much less liquid.', 'Between a stock and a 15-year lock-in government scheme, which is generally more liquid?', 'The stock — it can typically be sold and converted to cash within days, while a product with a long lock-in period restricts access to your money for years.'),
    (3, 8, 'Choosing Products', 3, 'Risk', 'Every financial product carries some form of risk — market risk (price fluctuation), credit risk (the issuer failing to pay), or inflation risk (a "safe" return that doesn''t keep up with rising prices). Choosing a product means understanding which type and level of risk you''re comfortable accepting for a given goal.', 'Can a "guaranteed return" product still carry risk?', 'Yes — it may be safe from market fluctuation or default, but if its return is lower than inflation, it still carries the risk of losing real purchasing power over time.'),
    (3, 8, 'Choosing Products', 4, 'Time Horizon', 'Time horizon — how long until you need the money — is one of the most important factors in choosing a product. Generally, longer time horizons can accommodate higher-risk, higher-growth-potential products, since there''s more time to recover from short-term downturns.', 'How should your comfort with a product''s short-term volatility generally relate to your time horizon for that specific goal?', 'Generally, the longer your time horizon, the more short-term volatility you can typically afford to accept, since there''s more time for the investment to recover and grow before you actually need the funds.'),
    (4, 1, 'Asset Allocation', 1, 'Asset Allocation', 'Asset allocation is the decision of how to split your money across different asset classes — stocks, bonds, gold, cash — based on your goals, timeline, and comfort with risk. It''s widely considered one of the biggest drivers of long-term investment outcomes, often mattering more than which specific stock or fund you pick within an asset class.', 'Why might asset allocation matter more than picking individual investments within a category?', 'Because how your money is split across asset classes (stocks vs bonds vs cash, for example) tends to drive a large share of your overall results — the broad mix often matters more than fine-tuning individual picks within one category.'),
    (4, 2, 'Portfolio Building', 1, 'Portfolio Building', 'Building a portfolio means assembling a deliberate combination of investments that work together toward your goals, rather than accumulating investments one at a time without an overall plan. A well-built portfolio reflects your timeline and risk tolerance as a whole, not just each individual holding in isolation.', 'What''s the difference between "having several investments" and "having a portfolio"?', 'A portfolio implies the investments were chosen deliberately to work together toward specific goals, while simply having several investments may just mean they were added one at a time without an overall plan.'),
    (4, 3, 'Diversification', 1, 'Diversification', 'Diversification means spreading your money across different investments so that a poor outcome in any single one doesn''t significantly damage your overall results. This can mean diversifying across asset classes, sectors, or even geographies — the goal is to avoid having too much riding on any one outcome.', 'Does diversification guarantee you won''t lose money?', 'No — diversification reduces the impact of any single investment performing poorly, but it doesn''t eliminate risk altogether or guarantee positive returns.'),
    (4, 4, 'SIP vs Lump Sum', 1, 'SIP vs Lump Sum', 'A Systematic Investment Plan (SIP) means investing a fixed amount at regular intervals (commonly monthly), while a lump sum means investing a large amount all at once. SIPs can help smooth out the impact of market ups and downs over time (since you buy at many different price points), while a lump sum invested early has more total time to compound — each approach has trade-offs depending on your situation and how much capital you have available at once.', 'If markets are volatile, why might investing through a SIP feel less stressful than investing a lump sum all at once?', 'Because a SIP spreads your purchases across many different price points over time, rather than risking that a single lump-sum investment happens to land right before a downturn.'),
    (4, 5, 'Goal-Based Investing', 1, 'Goal-Based Investing', 'Goal-based investing means choosing your investments based on a specific objective — a house down payment in 5 years, retirement in 30 — rather than investing generically. Each goal can have its own appropriate mix of investments, based on that goal''s specific timeline and importance.', 'Why might you choose different investments for a 3-year goal versus a 25-year goal, even with the same amount of money?', 'Because the appropriate level of risk and volatility differs based on timeline — a shorter goal generally needs more stability, while a longer goal can typically tolerate more short-term fluctuation in exchange for potential growth.'),
    (4, 6, 'Risk Profiling', 1, 'Risk Profiling', 'Risk profiling is the process of understanding how much investment risk you''re both willing and able to take, based on factors like your age, income stability, goals, and emotional comfort with seeing your investments fluctuate in value. It''s meant to guide how aggressive or conservative your overall investment approach should be.', 'Can two people with the same income have different risk profiles?', 'Yes — risk profile depends on more than income alone; factors like job stability, financial goals, timeline, dependents, and personal comfort with volatility all shape how much risk someone can reasonably take on.'),
    (4, 7, 'Portfolio Rebalancing', 1, 'Portfolio Rebalancing', 'Over time, different parts of your portfolio grow at different rates, which can shift your asset allocation away from what you originally intended — for example, if stocks grow quickly, they may end up making up a larger share of your portfolio than planned. Rebalancing means periodically adjusting your holdings back toward your intended allocation.', 'Why might a portfolio that started as 60% stocks and 40% bonds no longer be a 60/40 split a few years later, even without you adding or removing any money?', 'Because stocks and bonds typically grow at different rates — if stocks perform better, they can grow to represent a larger share of the total portfolio than originally intended, shifting the balance over time.'),
    (4, 8, 'Tax Basics', 1, 'Income Tax', 'Income tax is a tax on the income you earn — salary, business profits, rental income, and more — collected by the government and used to fund public services. In India, individuals can typically choose between two tax regimes (old and new) with different rates and deduction structures, and the right choice depends on your specific income and eligible deductions.', 'Does everyone in India pay income tax under the same rules?', 'Not exactly — taxpayers can generally choose between different tax regimes with different rate structures and deduction rules, and the better choice depends on individual circumstances.'),
    (4, 8, 'Tax Basics', 2, 'Capital Gains', 'Capital gains are the profit you make when you sell an investment — like stocks, mutual funds, or property — for more than you paid for it. As of mid-2026, long-term gains on equity shares and equity mutual funds (held more than 12 months) are taxed at 12.5% on amounts above a ₹1.25 lakh exemption per financial year, while short-term gains (held 12 months or less) on the same assets are taxed at 20%. Other asset types, like debt funds and property, follow different holding-period rules and rates.', 'Does holding an equity investment longer always reduce the tax you''ll eventually pay on it?', 'Generally, yes for equity — holding past 12 months shifts gains from the short-term rate (20%) to the long-term rate (12.5%), plus you get an annual exemption on the first ₹1.25 lakh of long-term gains. But rules differ for other asset types, so this isn''t universal across every investment.'),
    (4, 8, 'Tax Basics', 3, 'Tax-saving Investments', 'Certain investments offer a deduction on the amount you invest, reducing your taxable income for that year — commonly referred to broadly as 80C investments (after the relevant section of the older tax code), which include options like ELSS mutual funds, PPF, and certain insurance premiums, generally available only under the older tax regime. ELSS funds specifically combine this upfront deduction with equity-style growth potential, but come with a mandatory lock-in period.', 'Why might someone choose the old tax regime even if its tax rates look higher on paper?', 'Because the old regime allows various deductions (like 80C investments) that can lower their effective taxable income — depending on how much they''re eligible to deduct, the old regime might result in lower actual tax paid despite higher headline rates.'),
    (4, 8, 'Tax Basics', 4, 'Tax Planning Basics', 'Tax planning means legally arranging your finances — through eligible deductions, exemptions, and the timing of transactions — to reduce how much tax you owe, as opposed to tax evasion, which means illegally hiding income or falsifying information. Good tax planning fits within your broader financial goals rather than being driven purely by minimizing tax in isolation.', 'What''s the key difference between tax planning and tax evasion?', 'Tax planning uses legal methods (deductions, exemptions, proper timing) to reduce tax owed, while tax evasion involves illegally concealing income or providing false information — one is legitimate financial strategy, the other is a legal violation.'),
    (5, 1, 'Net Worth', 1, 'Net Worth', 'Net worth is the difference between everything you own (assets — cash, investments, property) and everything you owe (liabilities — loans, debts). It''s a single number that captures your overall financial position at a point in time, and tracking it over time is often a clearer measure of progress than tracking income alone.', 'Can someone with a high income have a low or even negative net worth?', 'Yes — if their liabilities (debt) are large relative to their assets, or if they spend most of their income rather than building assets, a high earner can still have low or negative net worth.'),
    (5, 2, 'Financial Independence', 1, 'Financial Independence', 'Financial independence generally means having enough accumulated wealth or passive income that you''re no longer dependent on active employment income to cover your living expenses. It doesn''t necessarily mean stopping work — it means work becomes optional rather than financially required.', 'Does financial independence necessarily mean someone has stopped working entirely?', 'No — financial independence means having the financial flexibility to choose whether to work, not a requirement to stop; many financially independent people continue working by choice.'),
    (5, 3, 'Cash Flow Management', 1, 'Cash Flow Management', 'Cash flow management is the practice of tracking and controlling the money coming in (income) versus going out (expenses), ensuring you consistently have enough left over to save and invest. Positive cash flow — earning more than you spend — is the basic precondition for building wealth over time.', 'Why is positive cash flow described as a "precondition" for building wealth, rather than just a nice-to-have?', 'Because without consistently spending less than you earn, there''s no surplus money available to save or invest in the first place — wealth-building fundamentally depends on having that gap to work with.'),
    (5, 4, 'Multiple Income Streams', 1, 'Multiple Income Streams', 'Relying on a single source of income (like one job) means your entire financial position depends on that one source continuing uninterrupted. Multiple income streams — a side business, rental income, dividends, freelance work — can reduce this dependency and accelerate wealth-building by adding additional inflows.', 'What''s the main financial risk of relying entirely on a single income source?', 'If that single source is disrupted (job loss, business downturn), your entire financial position is immediately affected, since there''s no other income to fall back on while you recover.'),
    (5, 5, 'Wealth Building Strategies', 1, 'Wealth Building Strategies', 'Systematic wealth building generally combines a few consistent habits: spending less than you earn, investing the difference regularly rather than sporadically, choosing investments aligned with your actual goals and timeline, and giving compounding enough time to work. There''s no single shortcut — the strategies that tend to work are the ones applied consistently over years, not just occasionally.', 'Why might consistency matter more than the specific investment chosen, for most people building wealth over time?', 'Because consistent saving and investing, sustained over many years, allows compounding to do most of the work — sporadic or short-lived effort, even in a "great" investment, generally can''t replicate the cumulative effect of steady, long-term contributions.'),
    (5, 6, 'Opportunity Cost', 1, 'Opportunity Cost', 'Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative you give up when you make a choice. Spending ₹50,000 on something today has an opportunity cost equal to what that ₹50,000 could have grown into if invested instead — a useful lens for evaluating spending and investment decisions, not just a theoretical concept.', 'If you choose to spend money today instead of investing it, what is the "opportunity cost" of that choice?', 'The potential future value that money could have grown into if it had been invested instead — opportunity cost is about what you give up by choosing one option over the available alternative.'),
    (5, 7, 'Behavioral Finance', 1, 'Behavioral Finance', 'Behavioral finance studies how psychological biases — like overconfidence, fear of loss, or following the crowd — actually influence financial decisions, often in ways that diverge from purely "rational" choices. Recognizing these patterns in yourself can help you catch decisions driven by emotion or bias before they become costly mistakes.', 'What''s the core idea behind behavioral finance, as opposed to assuming everyone makes perfectly rational financial decisions?', 'That real financial decisions are heavily influenced by psychological factors and biases, which can lead people to make choices that diverge from what would be considered purely rational or in their best long-term interest.'),
    (5, 8, 'Retirement Planning', 1, 'Retirement Planning', 'Retirement planning means estimating how much money you''ll need to sustain your desired lifestyle once you stop earning active income, and then systematically building toward that target over your working years. It typically requires accounting for inflation, since the cost of living decades from now will likely be significantly higher than it is today.', 'Why does inflation matter so much when estimating how much money you''ll need for retirement decades from now?', 'Because the cost of living will likely rise substantially over decades due to inflation, meaning the amount that feels sufficient today may not actually cover the same lifestyle by the time you retire — plans need to account for that future, higher cost.'),
    (6, 1, 'Insurance', 1, 'Health', 'Health insurance covers hospitalisation and treatment costs, protecting your savings from being wiped out by a medical emergency. As medical costs continue to rise, even a single serious hospitalisation without insurance can undo years of careful saving and investing.', 'Why might someone with substantial savings still need health insurance?', 'Because a major medical event can cost far more than many people expect, and paying out of pocket can quickly erode savings that took years to build — insurance protects that accumulated wealth from a single unpredictable event.'),
    (6, 1, 'Insurance', 2, 'Life', 'Life insurance — most commonly term insurance, which provides pure protection for a fixed period without an investment component — is designed to financially protect the people who depend on your income if something happens to you. The core idea is to replace your income for your dependents, not to build wealth for yourself; a common rule of thumb is choosing coverage as a multiple of your annual income, sized to what your dependents would actually need over time.', 'Who is term life insurance primarily meant to protect — you, or someone else?', 'Someone else — specifically the people who financially depend on you (family, dependents), since the payout only happens after your death and is meant to replace the income they''d lose.'),
    (6, 1, 'Insurance', 3, 'Vehicle', 'Vehicle insurance protects against the financial cost of accidents, theft, or damage to a vehicle you own, and is mandatory by law in India for at least basic third-party coverage. Beyond the legal minimum, the level of additional coverage you choose depends on the vehicle''s value and your own risk tolerance.', 'Is vehicle insurance only relevant if you''re worried about damaging your own car?', 'No — basic third-party vehicle insurance is legally required in India regardless of how careful you are, since it protects others (and you) from the financial impact of an accident involving your vehicle.'),
    (6, 1, 'Insurance', 4, 'Property', 'Property insurance covers risks like fire, theft, or structural damage to a home or other real estate you own. For a major asset like property, the relatively small cost of insurance is generally far less than the financial impact of an uninsured loss.', 'Why might insuring a high-value asset like property make sense even if a damaging event is unlikely?', 'Because the cost of the insurance premium is small relative to the potential financial loss if the unlikely event does happen — insurance exists specifically for protecting against low-probability, high-impact events.'),
    (6, 2, 'Estate Planning', 1, 'Estate Planning', 'Estate planning means deciding, in advance, how your assets will be distributed after your death — through tools like a will. Without clear estate planning, the distribution of your assets may be decided by default legal rules rather than your actual wishes, which can create confusion or disputes for your family.', 'What can happen to your assets if you don''t have a will or any estate plan?', 'Default legal succession rules typically determine how assets are distributed, which may not match what you would have actually wanted, and can sometimes lead to confusion or disputes among family members.'),
    (6, 3, 'Nomination', 1, 'Nomination', 'A nomination is naming a specific person to receive a particular asset (like a bank account, insurance policy, or mutual fund) directly upon your death, which can make the transfer faster and simpler than going through broader legal succession. It''s worth noting a nominee may sometimes act as a trustee for the actual legal heirs rather than automatically becoming the final owner, depending on the asset type and applicable law.', 'Does having a nominee on an account guarantee that person automatically and permanently owns the asset after your death?', 'Not necessarily — depending on the asset type, a nominee may simply be the person authorized to receive and temporarily hold the asset, while the actual legal ownership may still be determined by succession law or a will.'),
    (6, 4, 'Emergency Planning', 1, 'Emergency Planning', 'Emergency planning, in this context, goes beyond just having an emergency fund — it includes making sure your important documents (insurance policies, investment records, identification) are organized and accessible, and that your family knows where to find them and who to contact in a crisis.', 'Why might having insurance and savings not be fully helpful in an emergency if no one else knows about them?', 'Because if family members don''t know a policy or account exists, or can''t locate the necessary documents, the financial protection you arranged may go unused or be significantly delayed exactly when it''s needed most.'),
    (6, 5, 'Fraud & Cybersecurity', 1, 'Fraud & Cybersecurity', 'Financial fraud increasingly happens digitally — phishing messages, fake investment schemes promising unrealistic guaranteed returns, and impersonation of banks or officials. Protecting your wealth isn''t just about growing it; it also means being alert to attempts to take it through deception.', 'What''s a common red flag of an investment scam?', 'A promise of unusually high, "guaranteed" returns with little or no risk — legitimate investments virtually always involve some level of risk and uncertainty, so guarantees of high, risk-free returns are a major warning sign.'),
    (6, 6, 'Inflation Protection', 1, 'Inflation Protection', 'Inflation gradually reduces what a fixed amount of money can buy over time, which means simply holding cash or low-return assets long-term can quietly erode your wealth even if the number in your account never goes down. Protecting accumulated wealth means ensuring at least part of it is invested in things that have historically had the potential to grow faster than inflation over the long term.', 'Can wealth "shrink" in real terms even while the account balance stays the same or grows slightly?', 'Yes — if the growth rate is lower than the inflation rate, the actual purchasing power of that wealth decreases over time, even though the number itself hasn''t dropped.'),
    (6, 7, 'Tax Optimization', 1, 'Tax Optimization', 'Tax optimization, at this wealth-building stage, means structuring your investments and finances in legal, deliberate ways to minimize unnecessary tax — for example, being mindful of holding periods for capital gains, or using eligible deductions where they genuinely fit your financial plan, rather than chasing tax savings as the primary goal at the expense of sound financial decisions.', 'Should minimizing tax ever be the main reason to choose an investment?', 'Generally not as the primary driver — tax efficiency is a useful secondary consideration, but an investment should first make sense for your actual goals, timeline, and risk tolerance; chasing tax savings alone can lead to poor-fit investments.'),
    (6, 8, 'Wealth Preservation', 1, 'Wealth Preservation', 'Wealth preservation is the shift in mindset that often becomes more important once substantial wealth has been built — from purely focusing on growth, to also actively protecting what''s been accumulated against risks like market downturns, fraud, inadequate insurance, or poor planning. It doesn''t mean becoming overly conservative; it means being deliberate about which risks are worth taking and which aren''t, given how much there now is to lose.', 'Why might someone''s approach to risk reasonably change after they''ve built significant wealth, compared to when they were just starting out?', 'Because the consequences of a major loss are different — early on, there''s more time and less at stake to recover from setbacks, while later, protecting what''s already been built can become just as important as continuing to grow it.'),
    (7, 1, 'Advanced Portfolio Strategy', 1, 'Advanced Portfolio Strategy', 'At this level, portfolio strategy moves beyond basic diversification toward more deliberate structuring — considering correlations between assets (how they move relative to each other), factor exposures (like value vs. growth investing styles), and how different parts of a portfolio interact under various market conditions, rather than just holding a broad mix.', 'Why might two assets that seem "different" still not provide much real diversification benefit?', 'Because diversification benefit depends on how assets move relative to each other (their correlation) — if two seemingly different assets tend to rise and fall together under similar conditions, holding both doesn''t reduce risk as much as it might first appear to.'),
    (7, 2, 'Global Investing', 1, 'Global Investing', 'Global investing means extending a portfolio beyond domestic markets to include international stocks, bonds, or funds, with the goals of accessing growth opportunities not available domestically and reducing dependence on any single country''s economic performance. It also introduces additional considerations, like currency fluctuation risk.', 'What''s a risk specific to global investing that doesn''t apply to staying entirely within domestic investments?', 'Currency risk — since returns from foreign investments are affected not just by the investment''s own performance, but also by how the foreign currency''s value moves relative to your home currency.'),
    (7, 3, 'Alternative Investments', 1, 'REITs', 'Real Estate Investment Trusts allow exposure to income-generating commercial real estate through exchange-traded units, offering a way to add real estate exposure to a portfolio without the capital intensity and illiquidity of buying physical property directly.', 'What problem do REITs solve for investors who want real estate exposure but not the typical drawbacks of direct property ownership?', 'They remove the large capital requirement and illiquidity of buying physical property, while still providing exposure to real estate''s income and value characteristics, through a more accessible, tradeable instrument.'),
    (7, 3, 'Alternative Investments', 2, 'InvITs', 'Infrastructure Investment Trusts function similarly to REITs, but hold income-generating infrastructure assets (like roads or power transmission lines) instead of real estate, allowing investors to gain exposure to infrastructure project returns through tradeable units.', 'How is an InvIT conceptually similar to a REIT?', 'Both pool investor money into income-generating assets (infrastructure for InvITs, real estate for REITs) and issue tradeable units, allowing broader investor access to asset classes that would otherwise require large direct capital and management involvement.'),
    (7, 3, 'Alternative Investments', 3, 'Commodities', 'Commodities — gold, oil, agricultural products, industrial metals — are raw materials whose prices are influenced by global supply and demand dynamics, often moving somewhat independently of stock and bond markets, which is part of why they''re sometimes considered for portfolio diversification.', 'Why might commodities sometimes behave differently from stocks and bonds during the same market period?', 'Because commodity prices are driven primarily by physical supply and demand factors (production, weather, geopolitical events) rather than the same factors that drive stock and bond markets, so they can sometimes move independently of broader market trends.'),
    (7, 3, 'Alternative Investments', 4, 'Venture Capital (Introduction)', 'Venture capital involves investing in early-stage, often privately-held companies with high growth potential, in exchange for an ownership stake. It typically carries very high risk (many early-stage companies fail) alongside the potential for very high returns if a company succeeds, and is generally illiquid for long periods until an exit event (like an acquisition or public listing).', 'Why is venture capital generally considered both higher risk and lower liquidity than investing in publicly listed stocks?', 'Higher risk because early-stage companies have a meaningfully higher chance of failure than established public companies, and lower liquidity because there''s no active exchange to sell your stake — you''re typically locked in until a specific exit event occurs.'),
    (7, 4, 'Economic Cycles', 1, 'Economic Cycles', 'Economies tend to move through recurring phases — expansion (growth), peak, contraction (slowdown or recession), and trough (the low point before recovery begins) — collectively called the economic cycle. Different asset classes and sectors often perform differently across these phases, which is part of why some investors adjust their approach based on where they believe the economy currently stands.', 'Do all investments tend to perform the same way at every stage of the economic cycle?', 'No — different asset classes and sectors often respond differently to expansion versus contraction phases, which is why understanding the broader economic cycle can inform how a portfolio might be positioned.'),
    (7, 5, 'Reading Financial Statements', 1, 'Balance Sheet', 'A balance sheet is a snapshot, at a specific point in time, of what a company owns (assets), what it owes (liabilities), and the resulting difference (shareholders'' equity). It answers the question "what is this company''s financial position right now?"', 'Does a balance sheet show a company''s performance over a period of time, or its financial position at a single moment?', 'A single moment — a balance sheet is a snapshot as of a specific date, unlike an income statement, which covers performance over a period of time.'),
    (7, 5, 'Reading Financial Statements', 2, 'Income Statement', 'An income statement (also called a profit and loss statement) shows a company''s revenue, expenses, and resulting profit or loss over a specific period — a quarter or a year — answering the question "how did this company perform over this period?"', 'If the balance sheet is a snapshot, what would you call the income statement by comparison?', 'More like a video or a summary of activity over a period — it captures performance and changes (revenue, expenses, profit) across a span of time, rather than a single fixed moment.'),
    (7, 5, 'Reading Financial Statements', 3, 'Cash Flow', 'A cash flow statement tracks the actual movement of cash in and out of a company across operating, investing, and financing activities. It''s possible for a company to report a profit on its income statement while still facing cash flow problems, which is part of why this statement is reviewed separately.', 'Can a company be profitable on paper but still run into cash problems?', 'Yes — profit (as reported on the income statement) doesn''t always mean cash is actually available when needed; timing differences, like customers who haven''t yet paid, can create real cash flow challenges despite reported profitability.'),
    (7, 6, 'Market Psychology', 1, 'Market Psychology', 'Beyond individual investor psychology, markets as a whole can exhibit collective psychological patterns — periods of widespread optimism that push prices beyond what fundamentals might justify, or periods of widespread pessimism that push prices unusually low. Recognizing these broader patterns is different from, but related to, managing your own individual emotional biases.', 'How is market-wide psychology different from your own individual investment psychology?', 'Individual psychology is about your own personal biases and emotional reactions, while market psychology refers to collective behavior across many investors simultaneously, which can move overall prices in ways that may not reflect underlying fundamentals.'),
    (7, 7, 'Legacy & Generational Wealth', 1, 'Estate Transfer', 'Estate transfer is the process by which your assets pass to your chosen beneficiaries after your death, ideally guided by clear documentation (like a will) to ensure the transfer happens according to your actual wishes and with minimal complication for your family.', 'Why does having clear documentation matter for estate transfer, beyond just having assets to pass on?', 'Without clear documentation, the distribution of assets may default to general legal succession rules rather than your specific wishes, potentially creating confusion, delays, or disputes for the people you intended to benefit.'),
    (7, 7, 'Legacy & Generational Wealth', 2, 'Family Wealth', 'Family wealth refers to financial resources intended to benefit and potentially be passed across multiple generations, rather than being consumed entirely within one person''s lifetime. Building and preserving family wealth often requires different planning considerations than individual wealth-building alone.', 'How might planning for "family wealth" differ from planning purely for your own individual financial goals?', 'It involves a longer time horizon (potentially spanning multiple generations) and additional considerations like how wealth will be transferred, managed, and preserved across family members over time, not just accumulated for one person''s use.'),
    (7, 7, 'Legacy & Generational Wealth', 3, 'Philanthropy', 'Philanthropy — directing wealth toward charitable causes — can be part of a broader wealth strategy at this stage, sometimes offering both personal fulfillment and, depending on jurisdiction and structure, certain tax considerations, alongside its direct social impact.', 'Is philanthropy purely about giving money away, with no other financial considerations involved?', 'Not necessarily — while the primary motivation is typically social impact, philanthropic giving can sometimes also carry tax implications, which is part of why it''s often considered alongside other wealth planning decisions.'),
    (7, 7, 'Legacy & Generational Wealth', 4, 'Long-term Legacy', 'Long-term legacy planning considers the broader impact you want your accumulated wealth and decisions to have, beyond your own lifetime — encompassing estate transfer, family wealth, and philanthropy together as parts of a single, intentional long-term plan rather than separate afterthoughts.', 'Why might it make sense to think about estate transfer, family wealth, and philanthropy together, rather than as separate, unrelated decisions?', 'Because they''re all part of the same underlying question — what happens to your accumulated wealth and impact beyond your own lifetime — and decisions in one area (like estate structure) can directly affect outcomes in the others.'),
    (7, 8, 'Continuous Learning', 1, 'Reading Annual Reports', 'A company''s annual report provides detailed insight into its financial performance, strategy, and risks, going well beyond headline news coverage. Developing the habit of reading these reports for companies you''re invested in can deepen your understanding of what you actually own.', 'Why might an annual report give you more useful insight than a news headline about a company?', 'Because annual reports provide detailed, structured financial data and management commentary, while news headlines often capture only a brief, surface-level summary that may miss important context.'),
    (7, 8, 'Continuous Learning', 2, 'Following Markets', 'Staying informed about broader market trends and economic developments helps contextualize how your specific investments might be affected by larger forces, though it''s worth balancing this awareness against the earlier lesson on avoiding overreaction to short-term noise.', 'How does following markets relate back to the earlier idea of long-term thinking and avoiding emotional decisions?', 'Staying informed is useful for context and understanding, but it needs to be balanced against the discipline of not overreacting to every short-term market movement — awareness and emotional reactivity are not the same thing.'),
    (7, 8, 'Continuous Learning', 3, 'Macroeconomics', 'Macroeconomics studies large-scale economic factors — interest rates, inflation, employment, GDP growth — that affect markets broadly, beyond any single company or sector. A basic understanding helps make sense of why markets sometimes move significantly on news that isn''t about any specific company.', 'Why might a stock''s price drop on a day when no company-specific news was released about it?', 'Because broader macroeconomic news (like an interest rate change or inflation report) can affect investor sentiment and market conditions overall, impacting stock prices broadly, independent of any news specific to that individual company.'),
    (7, 8, 'Continuous Learning', 4, 'Building an Investment Philosophy', 'An investment philosophy is your own consistent, well-reasoned approach to making investment decisions — built from what you''ve learned, your goals, and your comfort with risk — that helps you make decisions confidently and consistently, rather than reacting differently to every new piece of information or market movement.', 'Why is having a personal investment philosophy useful, even after learning all the individual concepts covered in earlier levels?', 'Because knowledge alone doesn''t guarantee consistent decision-making — a clear, personal philosophy ties everything you''ve learned into a coherent framework you can rely on, helping you stay consistent rather than reacting differently each time new information or market movement appears.')
